WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.500
The shell tool can technically express many commands.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:14.500
Policy allows reading this repo but denies writes outside its workspace.

3
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:21.500
The tool list describes capability. The effective boundary determines which effects can occur.

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Policy evaluates the specific action before execution.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:36.167
A read may be allowed, publication may need approval and a forbidden destination is denied.

6
00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:43.167
Asking is not executing. Only an authorized branch reaches the effect.

7
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:50.667
Approval covers publishing diff A to the review branch.

8
00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:58.217
Changing the diff to B or the destination to main changes the scope of that decision.

9
00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:05.767
Bind the receipt to action, destination and version. An old “yes” does not authorize any replacement.

10
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:13.267
Policy checks the destination while it points to A.

11
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:20.433
Another process changes it before execution. The check no longer describes the current state.

12
00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:27.433
Prevent that race or revalidate the identity when applying the effect.

13
00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:34.933
The model requests an operation without needing to read its execution credential.

14
00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:41.933
A broker uses an identity limited to the authorized repository and action.

15
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:50.233
The secret stays outside the context and the credential has minimal scope. Policy is still required.

16
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:57.733
A repository file contains a sentence asking to bypass review.

17
00:01:57.733 --> 00:02:04.900
Treat that sentence as untrusted data, not a new harness rule.

18
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:12.817
Publication still goes through external control. An editable hook must not be the only defence.

